output:
`for…in`
 iterates over property names, not values (and did so in an unspecified order 
up until ES2020(https://262.ecma-international.org/10.0/#sec-enumerate-object-properties)
*). You shouldn’t use it to iterate over arrays. For them, there’s ES6’s 
`Array.prototype.entries`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/entries)
, which now has support across current browser versions:

```javascript
const myArray = [123, 15, 187, 32];

for (const [i, value] of myArray.entries()) {
  console.log(`${i}: ${value}`);
}

// 0: 123
// 1: 15
// 2: 187
// 3: 32
```

```javascript
.as-console-wrapper { max-height: 100% !important; top: 0; border-top: 0 !important; }
```

Or, for extended compatibility with older browsers, there’s ES5’s 
`forEach`
 method that passes both the value and the index to the function you give it:

```javascript
myArray.forEach(function (value, i) {
  console.log('%d: %s', i, value);
});
```

For iterables in general (where you would use a 
`for…of`
 loop rather than a 
`for…in`
), 
iterator helpers
 are now in the language. You can use 
`Iterator.prototype.forEach`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Iterator/forEach)
 to iterate over an entire iterable with an index:

```javascript
function* fibonacci() {
  let a = 0;
  let b = 1;

  for (;;) {
    yield a;
    [a, b] = [b, a + b];
  }
}

fibonacci().take(10).forEach((x, i) => {
  console.log(`F_${i} = ${x}`);
});
```

```javascript
.as-console-wrapper { max-height: 100% !important; top: 0; border-top: 0 !important; }
```

More generally, 
`Iterator#map`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Iterator/map)
 can associate the values yielded by an iterator with their indexes:

```javascript
fibonacci().map((x, i) => [i, x])
```

Not every iterable (or iterator!) is an 
`Iterator`
, but you can convert every iterable to an 
`Iterator`
 with 
`Iterator.from`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Iterator/from)
.

Without support for iterator helpers, you can use a generator function instead:

```javascript
function* enumerate(iterable) {
  let i = 0;

  for (const x of iterable) {
    yield [i, x];
    i+;
  }
}

for (const [i, obj] of enumerate(myArray)) {
  console.log(i, obj);
}
```

If you actually did mean 
`for…in`
 – enumerating properties – you would need an additional counter. 
`Object.keys(obj).forEach`
 could work, but it only includes 
own
 properties; 
`for…in`
 includes enumerable properties anywhere on the prototype chain.

* The order is still unspecified under 
certain circumstances(https://262.ecma-international.org/15.0/#sec-enumerate-object-properties)
, including for 
typed arrays
, proxies, and other exotic objects, as well as when properties are added or removed during iteration.

