There's no built-in ability to 
`break`
 in 
`forEach`
. To interrupt execution you would have to throw an exception of some sort. eg.

```javascript
var BreakException = {};

try {
  [1, 2, 3].forEach(function(el) {
    console.log(el);
    if (el === 2) throw BreakException;
  });
} catch (e) {
  if (e !== BreakException) throw e;
}
```

JavaScript exceptions aren't terribly pretty. A traditional 
`for`
 loop might be more appropriate if you really need to 
`break`
