Use 
`Array#some`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Core_JavaScript_1.5_Reference/Global_Objects/Array/some)

Instead, use 
`Array#some`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Core_JavaScript_1.5_Reference/Global_Objects/Array/some)
:

```javascript
[1, 2, 3].some(function(el) {
  console.log(el);
  return el === 2;
});
```

This works because 
`some`
 returns 
`true`
 as soon as any of the callbacks, executed in array order, return 
`true`
, short-circuiting the execution of the rest.

`some`
, its inverse 
`every`(https://developer.mozilla.org/en/Core_JavaScript_1.5_Reference/Global_Objects/Array/every)
 (which will stop on a 
`return false`
), and 
`forEach`
 are all ECMAScript Fifth Edition methods which will need to be added to the 
`Array.prototype`
 on browsers where they're missing.

